From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0000
Subject: drm/sun4i: de33: Add a writeback connector for the RT-WB unit

The H616 DE33 has a real-time write-back unit (RT-WB, DE + 0x10000; the
H616 User Manual lists "write back" among the display engine features and
gives no register description; the layout is the vendor disp2 de_wb.c of
the orange-pi-5.4-sun50iw9 BSP, de_lowlevel_v33x/de330). A mux in the DE
top block (display_clocks + 0x20) feeds it the output of one of the
blenders, the composed frame of a mixer before the TCON; it can scale the
frame down and convert it to YCbCr, and writes one frame per start into
memory. Nothing in the tree used it.

Expose it as a DRM writeback connector (sun50i_wb.c), so that the display
output can be verified on a board without a monitor: a KMS client commits
a writeback job with its frame, reads the captured frame back and compares
it. That is the missing check for the DE33 register latching (plane
blending, AFBC and 10-bit scanout, tearing: a torn frame shows two
patterns in one capture).

The unit shares the mixers' TCON vblank interrupt instead of an interrupt
of its own: the H616 has one DE interrupt that the mixer path does not
use, and one register read per frame is enough. A job is queued by the
connector's atomic_commit, after the planes of the commit are programmed
and latched; the mixer's vblank_quirk starts it right after it wrote the
latched registers for the frame, in the same blanking, so the capture
covers the first frame scanned out with the committed state. The next
vblank reads the FINISH status, signals the job's fence and starts the
next queued job; a start that does not finish within four frames fails
with -ETIMEDOUT, and the jobs of a CRTC that is turned off or re-modeset fail
with -ECANCELED (encoder atomic_disable). After each capture the unit is
stopped and reset, as the
vendor driver does (an idle unit left attached to the blender can stall
the mixer).

Output formats: XRGB/ARGB8888 and the other 32-bit RGB orders, RGB888,
BGR888, XRGB2101010 (no scaling or conversion on that path), NV12, NV21
and YUV420. The framebuffer may be smaller than the mode (coarse and
Lanczos2 fine down-scaling, vendor coefficients), up to the unit's 2048
pixel output line buffer. RGB to BT.601 limited-range YCbCr, or the
inverse on the TV encoder path where the blender works in YCbCr, uses the
DE33 CSC tables of sun8i_csc.c (same register layout as the layer CSC).

IOMMU: the RT-WB is a memory client of the display engine and uses its
IOMMU master (master 0, "DE", shared with the planes; H616 User Manual
table 3-8). The sun50i IOMMU attaches one domain at a time, so the node
has no iommus property of its own: the framebuffers it writes are
allocated by DRM through the planes device, the DRM DMA device, mapped in
that domain, and the addresses programmed are their IOVAs. Nothing is
bypassed.

The device tree node (writeback@10000) takes the CLK_WB gate and RST_WB
line of the DE33 clock block and refers to it for the routing register,
whose syscon range grows from 0x24 to 0x20 for that register. The
connector binds after the pipelines in sun4i_drv, once every CRTC exists,
and offers itself for the DE33 mixers (possible_crtcs), as a clone of
their output encoders.

Default behaviour is unchanged: the HDMI output, the latch and the DE2,
DE3 and A523 paths do not change; the connector is additional, and the
unit stays in reset until a job is queued. Read-only counters come as
/sys/module/sun8i_mixer/parameters/de33_wb_{jobs,done,failed,busy_frames,
status_last}.

References: LibreELEC 0235/0238/0240 (Jernej Skrabec, DE3 and DE33 RCQ
write-back) for the register names, the blender mux value, the scaler
programming and the shared DMA device.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
